--[[
The always-available standard-library namespace.

`nupp` is a table the prelude declares, so its fields are in scope everywhere
without a `require`; each implementation loads only if the checked program
actually uses it. A binding called `nupp` shadows it deliberately, the way a
binding called `string` would.

Its public namespaces are [](nupp.data) (JSON, UTF-8, UUIDs, hashes and
checksums), [](nupp.io) (buffers, byte views, readers, writers, and the typed
scalar cursors over them),
`nupp.io.Path` and `nupp.io.URI` (filesystem paths and resource identifiers),
[](nupp.math) (scalar helpers and two-dimensional vectors), `nupp.peg` (textual
parsing-expression grammars compiled at either phase), and `nupp.reflect`
(semantic type descriptors and reflection-driven keyed codecs).

Borrowing operations also live under this global: `nupp.borrow`,
`nupp.borrowFrom`, `nupp.region`, and `nupp.pin`. `nupp.region` is the unsafe audited
bridge from runtime-validated bounds to a checked child interval. Ownership itself is expressed by built-in
compile-time type-generator calls such as `affine(T, cleanup)`, `affine(T)`,
and `pinned(T)`, while `drop`, `unsafe adopt`, and `unsafe release` are language
operations over affine values.

This module's siblings are ordinary modules shipped with the compiler, reached
with `require` and separate from the fields of the ambient global: [](nupp.profile)
and the [](nupp.profile.zone) markers it reads, the `nupp.mem` storage family
(`nupp.mem.span`, `nupp.mem.heap`, `nupp.mem.soa`, and the `nupp.mem.indexed` range
they share), the `nupp.owners` custody pair ([](nupp.owners.set) and
[](nupp.owners.store)), [](nupp.io.file), and `nupp.compiler`, which holds the
self-hosted compiler implementation and is hidden from public API documentation by
its `@!internal` namespace root.

```nupp
local zone = require("nupp.profile.zone")
```
]]

const nupp = {}
return nupp
